  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through Friday)\nIssued at 1218 PM MDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nA Pacific low will approach slowly from over central CA on \nMonday, while a backdoor front makes westward progress into \neastern NM. Any convection that sparks Monday will be behind the \nbackdoor front and closer to the TX/OK borders where sufficient \nmoisture will reside. PWATs will ramp up dramatically Monday night\ninto Tuesday as a renewed Monsoon moisture plume stands up over \nthe region with the Pacific low on slow approach and the upper \nhigh centered over the TX coast. The Pacific low is forecast to \nfill and lift rapidly northeast toward the central/northern \nRockies on Wednesday as the upper high remains anchored along the\nupper Gulf coast, which will favor a continued stream of moisture\nnorthward into NM. PWATs on Tue/Wed are forecast to challenge \ndaily record values and the period is looking rather wet with \nwidespread soaking rainfall. A flash flood threat will ramp-up \nTue/Wed across much of the area and especially on/near area burn \nscars. The WPC has include much of our area in a slight risk for\nexcessive rainfall for Tue/Wed. Forecast uncertainty increases \nfrom Thursday onward, with the model consensus now favoring \nkeeping tropical system offshore of MX and south of the Baja \nPeninsula through Saturday as the upper high ridges westward over \nnorthern MX and keeps NM from realizing a tropical moisture fetch.\nThat said, south central, east central and southeast NM look to \nreceive the greatest rainfall amounts from Tue-Thu, with qpf\nranging from 2-3\" and locally as has high as 6\"!",
